#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068525 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05669abb-838d-5b1c-b97f-833a28b1d413" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Points in the Case of One or No Solutions"}] :question/id #uuid "7fb3c0cb-147c-5d02-a470-c86591394f2c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5192" :question/problem "Find the points of intersection, if any, of the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{x-2}+1$ and the line $y=-x+3.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{x-2}+1 \\\\ y=-x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-2}+1 & =-x+3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x-2} & =-x+2 \\\\ 1 & =(-x+2)(x-2) \\\\ 1 & =-{x}^{2}+4x-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}-4(1)(5)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{16-20}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{-4}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root of a negative number is not a real number, so there are no real solutions. Consequently, the line $y=-x+3$ does not intersect the reciprocal function $y=\\dfrac{1}{x-2}+1.$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/30eb7faffa9cdfbffedbbf0c9d48408fb1be1b8101da80864c4bced5d53d9903.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068526 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fafc8c8-0452-582f-b603-6f633de9a39a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Points in the Case of Two Solutions"}] :question/id #uuid "ffb2b712-52b9-5a09-9ac4-10a88f72f57e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5193" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/76c0e8aed74f2dcf3a382f79768098eda4f359631cdffb812a0aeccbb9707976.png)\n\nFind the points where the line $y=2x-3$ and the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{x-2}$ intersect (shown as the points $A$ and $B$ above)." :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=2x-3 \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{x-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal and then solving for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3 & =\\frac{1}{x-2} \\\\ (x-2)(2x-3) & =1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-7x+6 & =1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-7x+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(2x-5) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=1$ and $x=\\dfrac{5}{2}.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the first equation.\n\n- First, substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-3 \\\\  & =2(1)-3 \\\\  & =-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(1,-1).$\n\n- Then, substituting $x=\\dfrac{5}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-3 \\\\  & =2(\\frac{5}{2})-3 \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $\\left( \\dfrac{5}{2}, 2 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(1,-1)$ and $\\left( \\dfrac{5}{2}, 2 \\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068530 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2180b368-597c-5b99-a9ef-346169711637" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Parameter Given Two, One, or No Intersection Points"}] :question/id #uuid "b7b1d18b-256a-5aa4-9bcb-bd62e512231d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5260" :question/problem "The line $y=2x+3$ does not intersect the reciprocal function $y=\\dfrac{k}{x+3}$, where $k \\neq 0$. What is the range of possible values of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the line $y=2x+3$ intersects the reciprocal function $y=\\dfrac{k}{x+3},$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{k}{x+3} \\\\ y=2x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{x+3} & =2x+3 \\\\ k & =(2x+3)(x+3) \\\\ k & =2{x}^{2}+9x+9 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+9x+9-k & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-9\\pm \\sqrt{{9}^{2}-4(2)(9-k)}}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-9\\pm \\sqrt{81-72+8k}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-9\\pm \\sqrt{9+8k}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the line *not* to intersect the reciprocal function, the quadratic formula above must *not* give any real solutions.\n\nSo, the expression inside the square root must be negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9+8k & <0 \\\\ 8k & <-9 \\\\ k & <-\\frac{9}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k<-\\dfrac{9}{8}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353400 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fafc8c8-0452-582f-b603-6f633de9a39a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Points in the Case of Two Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b44763a-e684-562b-bbe0-5ce48508660f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353402} {:answer/id #uuid "5e583496-bf8a-589a-b156-0fa2450297f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353403} {:answer/id #uuid "96c40755-cb82-5adc-ad34-21e752738c9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353404} {:answer/id #uuid "fe9d5f04-670e-581f-a517-caf7ef897266" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353405} {:answer/id #uuid "f58e94f1-162c-567d-92c4-3dc0d8303ed1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96c40755-cb82-5adc-ad34-21e752738c9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353404} :answer-field/id #uuid "43db9e13-58ca-5cf2-bb56-368b7812fa4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353401}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8dbca007-d899-52ce-a942-d342784675f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-124047" :question/problem "The curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}-1$ intersects the line $y=2x$ at the points $(p,q)$ and $(r,s).$ What is $p+q+r+s?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{x}-1 \\\\ y=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x}-1 & =2x \\\\ \\frac{1-x}{x} & =2x \\\\ 1-x & =2{x}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+x-1 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(2x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=-1$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the second equation.\n\n- Substituting $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x \\\\  & =2(-1) \\\\  & =-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(-1,-2).$\n\n- Substituting $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $(\\frac{1}{2},1).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-1,-2)$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},1).$ \n\nFinally, if we let $p=-1,q=-2,r=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $s=1,$ then\n\n$$\np+q+r+s=-1-2+\\frac{1}{2}+1=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/0a2420b1e390edd61efd28a223cd1beb93db98609a8d31c69526434e7188658c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353407 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2180b368-597c-5b99-a9ef-346169711637" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Parameter Given Two, One, or No Intersection Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2865378e-353b-5726-90ac-e4d3575317a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353409} {:answer/id #uuid "48c48589-8aa8-5528-8614-ddd34d0a608d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353410} {:answer/id #uuid "e3e0964a-a117-55ff-98d6-c1e8d26996bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353411} {:answer/id #uuid "bc0a7f8c-2c3f-5c83-ad31-694028d51cf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353412} {:answer/id #uuid "f9d804b2-7657-57ae-aa50-df1a72da6569" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9d804b2-7657-57ae-aa50-df1a72da6569" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353413} :answer-field/id #uuid "991d1ef8-82d2-5e01-8d23-22d16d6cca08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d6311c5b-8c9a-5c5e-a71d-8fa36c325ce7" :question/math-academy-id "q-124166" :question/problem "The line $y=2x$ intersects the hyperbola $y=\\frac{k}{x-2},$ for $k\\ne 0,$ only once. What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the line $y=2x$ intersects the hyperbola $y=\\frac{k}{x-2},$  we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=2x \\\\ y=\\frac{k}{x-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{k}{x-2} \\\\ 2x(x-2) & =k \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4x-k & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-4)\\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}-4(2)(-k)}}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{16+8k}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the line to intersect the hyperbola only once, the quadratic formula above must give only one real solution.\n\nSo, the expression inside the square root must be equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16+8k & =0 \\\\ 8k & =-16 \\\\ k & =-\\frac{16}{8} \\\\ k & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $k=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353414 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2180b368-597c-5b99-a9ef-346169711637" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Parameter Given Two, One, or No Intersection Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b981ece2-4b8d-5566-9b5c-1ecae653988b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k\\in (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353416} {:answer/id #uuid "e55f9e77-30a5-5d0b-98c6-64a45b689dab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k\\in (0,2)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353417} {:answer/id #uuid "5d65e5eb-5529-5a3d-a08d-69ac6925baf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k\\in (0,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353418} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd3fd2e-d47b-5113-bf47-d3c0ff57b358" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353419} {:answer/id #uuid "a88d3f8e-a87e-5f24-be5a-eb3c1a0ebc60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k\\in (-\\infty ,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b981ece2-4b8d-5566-9b5c-1ecae653988b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k\\in (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353416} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fd38efc-8f47-570b-918d-740240f15546" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e0bd9754-4558-5a5b-aa2c-1054b22635af" :question/math-academy-id "q-124169" :question/problem "The line $y=x-k$ intersects the hyperbola $y=\\frac{k}{x}-k,$ where $k\\ne 0,$ twice. What is the range of possible values of  $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the line $y=x-k$ intersects the hyperbola $y=\\frac{k}{x}-k,$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:  \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=x-k &  \\\\ y=\\frac{k}{x}-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-k & =\\frac{k}{x}-k \\\\ x-k & =\\frac{k-kx}{x} \\\\ x(x-k) & =k-kx \\\\ {x}^{2} & =k \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the line to intersect the hyperbola at two points, the quadratic formula above must give two real solutions.\n\nSo, the expression inside the square root must be positive: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k\\in (0,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353421 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fafc8c8-0452-582f-b603-6f633de9a39a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Points in the Case of Two Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e03ef29-2c0e-545d-91a3-8a8615adce26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e03ef29-2c0e-545d-91a3-8a8615adce26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353423} :answer-field/id #uuid "358b2618-25b0-5827-84e2-6ec7dee891ef" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353422} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6de156a-4d78-5f07-b94f-b4b088714e9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353425}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6de156a-4d78-5f07-b94f-b4b088714e9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353425} :answer-field/id #uuid "e28d164b-210e-5e2d-b504-e8882b11a5b1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ff2d82e1-d4ad-5440-8ae8-351977e4748a" :question/math-academy-id "q-276446" :question/problem "The line $y=8x-1$ intersects the curve $y=\\frac{1}{2x}-1$ at the points $A(-\\frac{1}{4},-3)$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of $B.$\n\nThe coordinates of $B$ are $($\n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=8x-1 \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{2x}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x-1 & =\\frac{1}{2x}-1 \\\\ 8x & =\\frac{1}{2x} \\\\ 16{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the second equation.\n\n- Substituting $x=-\\frac{1}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =8x-1 \\\\  & =8(-\\frac{1}{4})-1 \\\\  & =-2-1 \\\\  & =-3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(-\\frac{1}{4},-3).$\n\n- Substituting $x=\\frac{1}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =8x-1 \\\\  & =8(\\frac{1}{4})-1 \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $(\\frac{1}{4},1).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $A(-\\frac{1}{4},-3)$ and $B(\\frac{1}{4},1).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/bb69eb0150879f215fe453e596ae18273ec149f1d1486c8d7dfe7b2c1e3e5e58.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353426 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fafc8c8-0452-582f-b603-6f633de9a39a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Points in the Case of Two Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b94b8b2-c40f-531b-bf52-46e6824ecd3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b94b8b2-c40f-531b-bf52-46e6824ecd3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353428} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bcb652a-0264-5e74-b69c-daf5ebf7841d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353427} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fff6902f-51c8-525b-89d0-0ea79086d519" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fff6902f-51c8-525b-89d0-0ea79086d519" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353430} :answer-field/id #uuid "a198454d-f94c-570f-b43f-34acd0894009" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353429} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db25c769-febc-5b75-8ce7-f7d80d6479d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db25c769-febc-5b75-8ce7-f7d80d6479d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353432} :answer-field/id #uuid "dce7f94f-8cca-5b0c-8ab2-4fcbfa4126c4" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353431} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "210806d3-ad65-5137-9b3c-d1ffad23c002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "210806d3-ad65-5137-9b3c-d1ffad23c002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353434} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d6f6e4e-cadd-512e-8bcb-16448b848b4a" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0cd54ca1-3fb2-5614-b9ee-7bc628e79ad6" :question/math-academy-id "q-276447" :question/problem "**From left to right,** the points of intersection of the curve $y=\\frac{2}{x}-5$ and the line $y=8x+1$ are $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of $A$ and $B.$\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}\n$),\\,B($\n{{field-3}},\n{{field-4}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{2}{x}-5 \\\\ y=8x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x}-5 & =8x+1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & =8x+6 \\\\ 2 & =8{x}^{2}+6x \\\\ 8{x}^{2}+6x-2 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+3x-1 & =0 \\\\ (4x-1)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{4},-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ and $x=-1.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the second equation.\n\n- Substituting $x=\\frac{1}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =8x+1 \\\\  & =8(\\frac{1}{4})+1 \\\\  & =2+1 \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(\\frac{1}{4},3).$\n\n- Substituting $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =8x+1 \\\\  & =8(-1)+1 \\\\  & =-8+1 \\\\  & =-7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $(-1,-7).$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the points of intersection are $A(-1,-7)$ and $B(\\frac{1}{4},3).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/5432e52f6099ec6e2d72ea6031e303eeaa0043129eea40baa064dd59b0d46520.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353435 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fafc8c8-0452-582f-b603-6f633de9a39a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Points in the Case of Two Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "835dfcfb-621a-5788-aa01-6f7ff6ad44ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353437} {:answer/id #uuid "a3eebeb8-c2a7-5bc4-a3e9-60afe939fadf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3eebeb8-c2a7-5bc4-a3e9-60afe939fadf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353438} :answer-field/id #uuid "65f70b1b-af72-59ee-bbed-98111ec3e8c0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353436} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "694915b7-44e6-5667-b6e2-7aa1b9018f3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "694915b7-44e6-5667-b6e2-7aa1b9018f3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353440} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7bcd62c-1aa6-55ca-a6ab-c2a495a02e16" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353439} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc38097e-3068-5bb3-93cc-03c373b036df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353442}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc38097e-3068-5bb3-93cc-03c373b036df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353442} :answer-field/id #uuid "4658616e-feae-543c-8e96-702868c6b930" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353441} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ffa37356-8622-5608-9eb9-b59ac67bc765" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353444}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffa37356-8622-5608-9eb9-b59ac67bc765" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353444} :answer-field/id #uuid "82ff66f3-2e39-52ec-96a7-3e6ba2d32d4a" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ac4ebe97-c33a-5aa8-8cba-aeb3f76762ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-276450" :question/problem "**From left to right,** the points of intersection of the curve $y=\\frac{4}{x-1}+2$ and the line $y-x-1=0$ are $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of $A$ and $B.$\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$),\\,B($\n{{field-3}},\n{{field-4}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we solve for $y$ in the second curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-x-1 & =0 \\\\ y & =x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{4}{x-1}+2 \\\\ y=x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{x-1}+2 & =x+1 \\\\ 4+2(x-1) & =(x+1)(x-1) \\\\ 2+2x & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x-3 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=-1$ and $x=3.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the second equation.\n\n- Substituting $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+1 \\\\  & =(-1)-1 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(-1,0).$\n\n- Substituting $x=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+1 \\\\  & =3+1 \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $(3,4).$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the points of intersection are $A(-1,0)$ and $B(3,4).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/6166b8bbae6ae1e528a72a5533e0e6906547e7e6076790cdb23306d11bfe9063.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353445 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2180b368-597c-5b99-a9ef-346169711637" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Parameter Given Two, One, or No Intersection Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbeb9607-540e-5b38-81e2-dc83a3923c10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{29}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353447} {:answer/id #uuid "d49701b5-0844-503e-af4a-5a22e5c6953c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbeb9607-540e-5b38-81e2-dc83a3923c10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{29}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353447} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e98764e-75b8-5961-a63c-2b3e568043d7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353446}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "12cfca36-2a8c-5aa4-9ec4-72deb70147e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-276454" :question/problem "The line $y=4x-5$ intersects the hyperbola $y=\\frac{k+5}{x}+1$, where $k\\ne -5,$  only once. What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the line $y=4x-5$ intersects the hyperbola $y=\\frac{k+5}{x}+1$,  we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:      \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=4x-5 &  \\\\ y=\\frac{k+5}{x}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-5 & =\\frac{k+5}{x}+1 \\\\ 4x-6 & =\\frac{k+5}{x} \\\\ (4x-6)x & =k+5 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-6x-k-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-6)\\pm \\sqrt{(-6)^{2}-4(4)(-k-5)}}{2(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pm \\sqrt{36+16k+80}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pm \\sqrt{116+16k}}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the line to intersect the hyperbola only once, the quadratic formula above must give only one real solution.\n\nSo, the expression inside the square root must be equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}116+16k & =0 \\\\ 16k & =-116 \\\\ k & =-\\frac{116}{16} \\\\ k & =-\\frac{29\\cdot 4}{4\\cdot 4} \\\\ k & =-\\frac{29}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the value of $k$ is $-\\frac{29}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353449 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05669abb-838d-5b1c-b97f-833a28b1d413" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Points in the Case of One or No Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b334e3ee-2bd7-56bc-95c6-e529c9335614" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353451} {:answer/id #uuid "67c3f372-db60-5d3b-b0f6-3b7e4264b418" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b334e3ee-2bd7-56bc-95c6-e529c9335614" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353451} :answer-field/id #uuid "6578b694-5677-5348-b9e2-f194845b88ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353450} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08f804a2-2cb6-54f1-a44d-6095b1e6a53c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08f804a2-2cb6-54f1-a44d-6095b1e6a53c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353454} :answer-field/id #uuid "26473e1e-b44d-5c32-8de0-75c8c7170d45" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "32c28e87-d455-5d16-b110-f34103372724" :question/math-academy-id "q-277383" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of $A$ which is the point of intersection of the curve $y=\\frac{5}{3x-2}+2$ and the line $y=4.$\n\nThe coordinates of $A$ are $($\n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{5}{3x-2}+2 \\\\ y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3x-2}+2 & =4 \\\\ \\frac{5}{3x-2} & =2 \\\\ 5 & =6x-4 \\\\ 9 & =6x \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$ To find the $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we plug this value into either curve. Let's choose the second equation.\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ we get $y=4.$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $A=(\\frac{3}{2},4).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/f07fc53462af6fbb7de0a901ae23721a2eb84f6e83bd5577a2659c175519fc67.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353455 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05669abb-838d-5b1c-b97f-833a28b1d413" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Points in the Case of One or No Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16a0bdb8-f5bb-54ba-b852-975fdb1373f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353457}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16a0bdb8-f5bb-54ba-b852-975fdb1373f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353457} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e55454a-0b79-5ea6-b864-fb6936097e11" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353456} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd56dcc3-0b21-55f2-ba30-ce3a18d41dbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd56dcc3-0b21-55f2-ba30-ce3a18d41dbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353459} :answer-field/id #uuid "45e7b644-0a0a-53d2-bfff-ff46b0c942e0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b11e759f-ff0f-5f2b-84fc-097bfa970897" :question/math-academy-id "q-277386" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of $A$ which is the point of intersection of the curve $y=\\frac{2}{2x+3}$ and the line $y=1.$\n\nThe coordinates of $A$ are $($\n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{2}{2x+3} \\\\ y=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{2x+3} & =1 \\\\ 2 & =2x+3 \\\\ -1 & =2x \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ To find the $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we plug this value into either curve. Let's choose the second equation.\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we get $y=1.$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $A=(-\\frac{1}{2},1).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/192f47788ac2bc716c4f3e0d8fa9efe9b2c26dd9e32bb9296cfcb00328e5db05.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353460 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2180b368-597c-5b99-a9ef-346169711637" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Parameter Given Two, One, or No Intersection Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "948c1111-14bb-55ba-87ff-a3dc7dd4cefb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-4\\sqrt{2}$ or $4\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353462} {:answer/id #uuid "d8181938-1fad-56b0-b232-980f7396e09c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\sqrt{2}$ or $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353463} {:answer/id #uuid "81ba21df-f1e3-5d0f-a1cc-816516151648" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-8-8\\sqrt{2}$ or $-8+8\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353464} {:answer/id #uuid "0a168de5-5b03-5edf-a2f6-178a101dbfbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-4-4\\sqrt{2}$ or $-4+4\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353465} {:answer/id #uuid "81858d3a-ca21-54d5-bc98-af2680576a7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2-2\\sqrt{2}$ or $-2+2\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a168de5-5b03-5edf-a2f6-178a101dbfbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-4-4\\sqrt{2}$ or $-4+4\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353465} :answer-field/id #uuid "e712c2e3-9e1c-57a9-bfbd-3a15d1f17767" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "17f6bb74-ea59-5538-b3d8-73cf1622119d" :question/math-academy-id "q-277432" :question/problem "The line $y=4x-k$ intersects the hyperbola $y=-\\frac{2}{x+1}$ only once. What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the line $y=4x-k$ intersects the hyperbola $y=-\\frac{2}{x+1},$  we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=4x-k \\\\ y=-\\frac{2}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-k & =-\\frac{2}{x+1} \\\\ (4x-k)(x+1) & =-2 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+4x-kx-k+2 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+(4-k)x+(2-k) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(4-k)\\pm \\sqrt{(4-k)^{2}-4(4)(2-k)}}{2(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(k-4)\\pm \\sqrt{16-8k+{k}^{2}-32+16k}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{(k-4)\\pm \\sqrt{{k}^{2}+8k-16}}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the line to intersect the hyperbola only once, the quadratic formula above must give only one real solution.\n\nSo, the expression inside the square root must be equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}+8k-16 & =0 \\\\ k & =\\frac{-8\\pm \\sqrt{(8)^{2}-4(1)(-16)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8\\pm \\sqrt{64+64}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8\\pm \\sqrt{64\\cdot 2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8\\pm 8\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-4\\pm 4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $k=-4-4\\sqrt{2}$ or $k=-4+4\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069194 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fafc8c8-0452-582f-b603-6f633de9a39a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Points in the Case of Two Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d34c6175-9688-5059-8fa8-1b0ffbdc2ad3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066696} {:answer/id #uuid "ec84c005-b0c4-5f24-8f20-1b900e2c2aa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066697} {:answer/id #uuid "fbe6d6b7-a4dd-51af-929f-92316a34a023" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066698} {:answer/id #uuid "374f6324-c4e9-594d-ac57-d6d7acd8d790" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066699} {:answer/id #uuid "6b8e032d-580c-597d-a6a7-fecffef9e5fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbe6d6b7-a4dd-51af-929f-92316a34a023" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066698} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f44e5bc-fae7-5b9f-b732-de61d0f1c8be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a9bdc8c8-a628-5a28-9c53-49f974293c50" :question/math-academy-id "q-3471" :question/problem "The curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}$ intersects the line $4y-5x=8$ at the points $(p,q)$ and $(r,s).$ What is $p+q+r+s?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we solve for $y$ in the second curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-5x & =8 \\\\ 4y & =5x+8 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5x+8}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ y=\\frac{5x+8}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{5x+8}{4} \\\\ 4 & =5{x}^{2}+8x \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+8x-4 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(5x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=-2$ and $x=\\frac{2}{5}.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the first equation.\n\n- Substituting $x=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n- Substituting $x=\\frac{2}{5},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\nFinally, if we let $p=-2,q=-\\frac{1}{2},r=\\frac{2}{5}$ and $s=\\frac{5}{2},$ then\n\n$$\np+q+r+s=-2-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/d995288e573a22db67c7d4c82c1a12bcbb41fd880f706b9fb62062aa90e2d5ba.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069256 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05669abb-838d-5b1c-b97f-833a28b1d413" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Points in the Case of One or No Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ffa3959-bcae-53d4-b750-3acd046f4817" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067068} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f13b0a-3767-5aac-9371-497ed9cc05f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067069} {:answer/id #uuid "15c4250c-ec22-5c0f-9869-16bf0a21f119" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067070} {:answer/id #uuid "76e00e67-6b8c-5712-9e4a-778c86b82f69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined because the curve and the line do not intersect." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067071} {:answer/id #uuid "7c894e31-8bab-5c7b-bdc4-511f737f9090" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76e00e67-6b8c-5712-9e4a-778c86b82f69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined because the curve and the line do not intersect." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067071} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5ce676b-13f0-56a5-846b-a845f49e5220" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "801b9317-386e-554f-9207-4d232469922d" :question/math-academy-id "q-41957" :question/problem "If the line $y=-2x+3$ and the curve $y=\\frac{2}{x-2}+1$ intersect at the point $(p,q),$ then $p+q=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-2x+3 &  \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{x-2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+3 & =\\frac{2}{x-2}+1 \\\\ -2x+2 & =\\frac{2}{x-2} \\\\ (-2x+2)(x-2) & =2 \\\\ 2(-x+1)(x-2) & =2 \\\\ (-x+1)(x-2) & =1 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+3x-2 & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{{3}^{2}-4(1)(3)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{9-12}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{-3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root of a negative number is not a real number, so there are no real solutions. Consequently, the line $y=-2x+3$ does not intersect the hyperbola $y=\\frac{2}{x-2}+1.$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/41fee04552a47d93f1dbd788e69e12c4b58e377ec4c0b63c3ed55c712d337f94.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2180b368-597c-5b99-a9ef-346169711637" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Parameter Given Two, One, or No Intersection Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af313f5e-a005-5414-9c3f-4d13f0de9fc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067080} {:answer/id #uuid "e5370b69-9d55-5f27-a7a2-0ad1256a6e4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067081} {:answer/id #uuid "ec209266-34c7-5a87-b135-e206517b7b36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067082} {:answer/id #uuid "43e9fe84-11e3-5629-85b3-37ed78b241d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067083} {:answer/id #uuid "6514292f-e62c-59c5-96fa-4f8d6a14fd23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43e9fe84-11e3-5629-85b3-37ed78b241d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067083} :answer-field/id #uuid "cce59308-6af8-5c9f-809a-6ff0d4874a80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d169426d-f449-5cba-831a-9928b82fcb52" :question/math-academy-id "q-41970" :question/problem "The line $y=2x+4$ intersects the hyperbola $y=-\\frac{k+1}{x}+1$, where $k\\ne -1$, only once. What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the line $y=2x+4$ intersects the hyperbola $y=-\\frac{k+1}{x}+1$,  we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=2x+4 &  \\\\ y=-\\frac{k+1}{x}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4 & =-\\frac{k+1}{x}+1 \\\\ 2x+3 & =-\\frac{k+1}{x} \\\\ (2x+3)x & =-(k+1) \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+3x+k+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{{3}^{2}-4(2)(k+1)}}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{9-8k-8}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{1-8k}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the line to intersect the hyperbola only once, the quadratic formula above must give only one real solution.\n\nSo, the expression inside the square root must be equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-8k & =0 \\\\ 8k & =1 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $k=\\frac{1}{8}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353467 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05669abb-838d-5b1c-b97f-833a28b1d413" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Points in the Case of One or No Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2529c161-bb63-5f3c-bba5-7f42a5838428" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined because the curve and the line do not intersect" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353469} {:answer/id #uuid "b9713aaf-03f8-5c60-bcd5-6c5f343e3fae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353470} {:answer/id #uuid "ad302334-1f56-5429-9a32-61aa7d357a43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353471} {:answer/id #uuid "97ae121c-7f47-5f1e-aaa1-0b2e0d79d65a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353472} {:answer/id #uuid "d555ef9a-7c96-5671-99d5-dfe9bc6d2564" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2529c161-bb63-5f3c-bba5-7f42a5838428" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined because the curve and the line do not intersect" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353469} :answer-field/id #uuid "7218e9fa-63cc-502f-83b2-98734d4e6796" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4e865414-12bd-52fe-8279-144361b0cdeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-70457" :question/problem "If the line $y=-2x-1$ and the curve $y=\\frac{3}{x+3}+1$ intersect at the point $(p,q),$ then $p+q=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-2x-1 &  \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{x+3}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x-1 & =\\frac{3}{x+3}+1 \\\\ -2x-2 & =\\frac{3}{x+3} \\\\ (-2x-2)(x+3) & =3 \\\\ -2{x}^{2}-8x-6 & =3 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+8x+9 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-8\\pm \\sqrt{{8}^{2}-4(2)(9)}}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8\\pm \\sqrt{64-72}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8\\pm \\sqrt{-8}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root of a negative number is not a real number, so there are no real solutions. Consequently, the line $y=-2x-1$ does not intersect the hyperbola $y=\\frac{3}{x+3}+1.$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/cae5448d236e7e07bd2edf2281f495703b90b2cb3a160bd3d1a18e5915a8abaf.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353474 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05669abb-838d-5b1c-b97f-833a28b1d413" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Points in the Case of One or No Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b628eb9-cf4f-55c5-8c48-979f75d80415" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined because the curve and the line do not intersect" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353476} {:answer/id #uuid "388de875-d05f-57b1-9b93-9d39c7a5f4fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353477} {:answer/id #uuid "1c19200d-6e64-5b17-8fab-12f34418d980" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353478} {:answer/id #uuid "3968aa87-cbe2-51d9-817a-23952f5dec1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353479} {:answer/id #uuid "50890ad8-ffe5-5f1f-b84a-187ba2c484ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b628eb9-cf4f-55c5-8c48-979f75d80415" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined because the curve and the line do not intersect" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353476} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b2b3195-f4b1-5cc5-972c-eb4e1f760eee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8eeeeb33-370e-55f8-9167-b74825d5d39a" :question/math-academy-id "q-70463" :question/problem "If the line $y=-x+5$ and the curve $y=\\frac{2}{x-5}+2$ intersect at the point $(p,q),$ then $p+q=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-x+5 &  \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{x-5}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+5 & =\\frac{2}{x-5}+2 \\\\ -x+3 & =\\frac{2}{x-5} \\\\ (-x+3)(x-5) & =2 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+8x-15 & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+17 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-8)\\pm \\sqrt{(-8)^{2}-4(1)(17)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pm \\sqrt{64-68}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pm \\sqrt{-4}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root of a negative number is not a real number, so there are no real solutions. Consequently, the line $y=-x+5$ does not intersect the hyperbola $y=\\frac{2}{x-5}+2.$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/73570088dd6c424f4848b25ce3305edaf1ec50c07039b463f4af9a5dd35bfb43.png)"}]}
